var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Contents","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Narcissus","page":"Reference","text":"Narcissus\n\nA terminal UI for looking at Julia objects: point it at a value and walk its fields, elements and entries, one level at a time.\n\nusing Narcissus\nnarcissus(my_object)\n\nBuilt on Tachikoma.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Narcissus"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.BINDING_KINDS","page":"Reference","text":"The filter categories f cycles through, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.BINDING_KINDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.CLIPBOARD_COMMANDS","page":"Reference","text":"CLIPBOARD_COMMANDS\n\nCommands tried, in order, to put text on the system clipboard.\n\nTachikoma's own clipboard_copy! knows only xclip, which is not installed on a great many Linux desktops — Wayland sessions use wl-copy — and it swallows the failure, so the app cheerfully reports a copy that never happened. Trying the alternatives and reporting the truth is worth the twenty lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.CLIPBOARD_COMMANDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.DEFAULT_LIMIT","page":"Reference","text":"Default number of children materialised per container before eliding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.DEFAULT_LIMIT"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.DETAIL_LOOKAHEAD","page":"Reference","text":"Extra lines rendered beyond the visible window, so a short scroll is free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.DETAIL_LOOKAHEAD"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.EQUALITY_BUDGET","page":"Reference","text":"How many component comparisons a single equality test may cost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.EQUALITY_BUDGET"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.EQUALITY_DEPTH","page":"Reference","text":"How deep a structural comparison may recurse before giving up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.EQUALITY_DEPTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.MAX_IN_FLIGHT","page":"Reference","text":"How many measurements may be in flight at once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.MAX_IN_FLIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.MEMORY_COLUMN_WIDTH","page":"Reference","text":"MEMORY_COLUMN_WIDTH\n\nWidth of the memory column: 1023 KiB ▕████····▏ 100%. Fixed, because the column is right-aligned to the pane and a variable width would stop the sizes and percentages lining up — which is the entire reason to look at them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.MEMORY_COLUMN_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.OBJECT_HEADER","page":"Reference","text":"Julia's per-object tag word, which sizeof does not include.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.OBJECT_HEADER"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.SCAN_BUDGET","page":"Reference","text":"How many elements of an array to scan before taking its word for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.SCAN_BUDGET"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.SEARCH_BUDGET","page":"Reference","text":"SEARCH_BUDGET\n\nHow many nodes a deep search may visit before giving up.\n\nThe walk materialises every node it touches and renders every leaf it tests, so this bounds both how long a search can block the UI and how much tree it can grow behind your back. Warm, a full budget costs on the order of 70ms; the first walk over a shape of object never seen before is slower, and that is Julia compiling the decomposition, not the search.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.SEARCH_BUDGET"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.SIZE_BUDGET","page":"Reference","text":"SIZE_BUDGET\n\nHow many distinct objects a size measurement may visit before giving up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.SIZE_BUDGET"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Absent","page":"Reference","text":"Absent\n\nThe stand-in for a component that exists on only one side of a Diff: a key one dictionary has and the other does not, or the tail of the longer of two arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Absent"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.AccessError","page":"Reference","text":"AccessError(err)\n\nPlaceholder for a component whose value could not be retrieved — getindex or getfield threw. Keeps the exploration alive instead of taking the app down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.AccessError"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Component","page":"Reference","text":"Component(key, template, value; kind=:index)\n\nOne part of a decomposed value, as returned by components. A row in the tree is built out of exactly these four things:\n\n├─▸ weights::Matrix{Float64} = [0.1 0.2; 0.3 0.4]\n    └ key    └ from `value`    └ from `value`\n\nkey — the label in the left column\n\nShort, and specific enough to tell siblings apart. The built-in conventions, worth following so a custom type does not look foreign next to a Vector:\n\nContainer key\nstruct field the bare name, \"weights\"\nsequence \"[3]\", or \"[2, 5]\" for a multidimensional index\ndictionary the key as Julia code, \":name\" or \"\"host\"\" — i.e. repr(k)\n\nIt shares a row with the type and the value, so keep it to a few characters where you can. It is also what / searches, along with the type and the rendered value.\n\ntemplate — the path expression\n\nThe Julia expression that reaches this component from its parent, with {} standing in for the parent's own path: \"{}.weights\", \"{}[3]\", \"{}[:name]\", \"collect({})[2]\". Paths compose as the tree descends, so a component three levels down ends up as model.layers[2].weights, and that is what y puts on the clipboard — make it something that can be pasted into the REPL and evaluated.\n\nWhen no expression reaches the component — a set member, say — use \"{}\", so the path names the parent rather than lying about the child.\n\nvalue — the thing itself\n\nWhatever the component holds. Two stand-ins exist for the awkward cases, and using them is much better than throwing:\n\nUndef for a field that is declared but not assigned.\nAccessError for a value that could not be retrieved.\n\nkind — how the key is coloured\n\n:field, :index or :key. Purely cosmetic: it tells the reader at a glance whether they are looking at a struct, a sequence or a mapping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Component"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Diff","page":"Reference","text":"Diff(x, y)\n\nA pair of values under comparison. Narcissus treats it as a value in its own right: it decomposes by zipping the components of x and y together, so the whole explorer — lazy expansion, elision of long containers, cycle detection, and the semantic/field mode toggle — works on a comparison unchanged.\n\nWhatever you leave the cursor on when you quit narcissus(x, y) comes back as one of these; reach the two sides through .x and .y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Diff"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Differing","page":"Reference","text":"Differing(node)\n\nA view of an ObjNode whose children are only the ones that differ. Wrapping rather than filtering in place keeps the tree itself intact — the same node can be printed whole a moment later.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Differing"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.ExplorationMode","page":"Reference","text":"ExplorationMode\n\nHow a value should be taken apart. Two modes ship with Narcissus:\n\nSemantic — what the value means: a Dict's entries, an array's elements, a set's members.\nFields — what the value is: the raw struct fields Julia stores, so a Dict becomes slots, keys, vals, ndel, … and a Vector becomes ref and size.\n\nEvery node in the explorer carries its own mode, toggled with m, and the nodes opened underneath it inherit it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.ExplorationMode"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Fields","page":"Reference","text":"The storage of a value: its raw struct fields. See ExplorationMode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Fields"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.ObjNode","page":"Reference","text":"ObjNode\n\nA node in the exploration tree. Children are materialised only when a node is first expanded, so opening the explorer on a deeply nested (or self referential, or huge) object costs nothing until you actually walk into it.\n\nEach node also carries its own ExplorationMode: a Dict can be shown as its entries or as its raw fields, and the nodes opened underneath it inherit whichever was chosen.\n\nkind is one of:\n\n:root    — the object handed to narcissus\n:field   — a struct field\n:index   — an array/tuple/set element\n:key     — a dictionary entry\n:cycle   — a value already present among this node's ancestors\n:elided  — the … N more stand-in for a truncated container\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.ObjNode"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.ObjectTree","page":"Reference","text":"ObjectTree(root)\n\nTachikoma widget rendering an ObjNode tree. Unlike a plain TreeView it colours each part of a row separately — key, type, value — and pulls children in only as nodes are opened.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.ObjectTree"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Row","page":"Reference","text":"One visible line of the tree, with the ancestry needed to draw connectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Row"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Semantic","page":"Reference","text":"The meaning of a value: entries, elements, members. See ExplorationMode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Semantic"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.Undef","page":"Reference","text":"Undef\n\nPlaceholder for a struct field that is declared but not assigned (isdefined(x, i) === false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.Undef"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._ansi-Tuple{Tachikoma.Style}","page":"Reference","text":"Theme colour as something printstyled accepts (it takes 0:255 directly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._ansi"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._docs!-Tuple{Any, Any, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"_docs!(spans, value, width; separator=true)\n\nAppend the value's rendered documentation, or a note that there is none.\n\nWhat you want from a function, a type or a method is almost never its show output — it is what the thing is for. The Markdown comes back as ANSI, which parse_ansi splits into styled spans, so the pane shows headers, emphasis and code blocks rather than a wall of asterisks.\n\nA value that has nothing of its own to say borrows from the name it shares — see doc_object — and the borrowing is labelled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._docs!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._method_key-Tuple{Method}","page":"Reference","text":"_method_key(m::Method) -> String\n\nA method's argument signature, (::Int64, ::String), with the function's own type dropped — it is the same for every row and the tree already says which function you are looking at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._method_key"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._shallow_size-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"The object's own storage, before anything it points at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._shallow_size"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._supertype_string-Tuple{Type}","page":"Reference","text":"supertype, or an empty string for the types that do not have one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._supertype_string"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus._tokens-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"Split text into alternating runs of spaces and non-spaces, keeping both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus._tokens"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.anomaly-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"anomaly(x) -> Union{Nothing,Symbol}\n\nWhether x is the sort of value you opened the explorer to find, and which sort. nothing means \"looks fine\".\n\nSymbol Value\n:nan a NaN, or an array containing one\n:inf an Inf, or an array containing one\n:missing a missing, or an array containing one\n:empty an empty container\n:undef a declared but unassigned field\n:error a value that could not be retrieved\n\nnothing and missing-typed containers are not anomalies in themselves — they are too ordinary to be worth flagging, and flagging the ordinary is how a warning marker stops being read.\n\nArrays are scanned, which is why the answer is cached per node: the scan is capped at SCAN_BUDGET elements, beyond which a large array is taken at face value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.anomaly_marker-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Symbol}}","page":"Reference","text":"Row glyph and style for an anomaly; nothing draws nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.anomaly_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.anomaly_state-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"anomaly_state(node) -> Symbol\n\n:pending if nobody has looked yet, :none if the value is fine, otherwise the anomaly.\n\nThe renderer uses this rather than node_anomaly so that drawing a frame never scans a million-element array: it draws what is known and asks for the rest to be worked out off the main task.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.anomaly_state"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.append_children!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"Append the window of children starting at start, plus an elision marker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.append_children!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.binding_kind-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"binding_kind(x) -> Symbol\n\nWhich drawer of a module a binding belongs in: :module, :type, :function or :value. What f filters a module's listing by, since \"show me the types this package defines\" is a different question from \"show me everything\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.binding_kind"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.bounded_size-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"bounded_size(x; budget=SIZE_BUDGET) -> (bytes, truncated)\n\nRetained size of x, counting shared objects once, with a hard cap on how far it will look.\n\nBase.summarysize answers the same question but cannot be told to stop, and walks into runtime machinery that is not the caller's data — see skip_for_size. This walk is bounded and refuses those, so the memory view cannot be made to hang the app by pointing it at the wrong row. When the budget runs out the result is reported as a lower bound rather than a number pretending to be exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.bounded_size"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.byte_size-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"byte_size(x) -> Int\n\nRetained size of x in bytes — see bounded_size, whose budget this inherits. A lower bound for anything too large to finish measuring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.byte_size"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.bytes_state-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"-1 while nobody has measured this node yet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.bytes_state"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.clipboard_hint-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"Name the tools that could have taken the text, for a useful failure message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.clipboard_hint"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.compact_show-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"compact_show(v; width, height) -> String\n\nshow(v) under :compact and :limit, flattened to one line and clipped. Never throws: a broken show method becomes a visible marker instead of a crashed explorer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.compact_show"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.comparable-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"comparable(a, b) -> Bool\n\nWhether two values are similar enough to line their components up. Values of differently named types are not: Foo against Bar is reported as a type change rather than zipped field by field. Type parameters may differ freely, since Foo{Int} and Foo{Float64} still have the same fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.comparable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.comparing-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer}","page":"Reference","text":"Whether this explorer is comparing two objects rather than exploring one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.comparing"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.comparing-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"Whether this tree is comparing two objects rather than exploring one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.comparing"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.component_count","page":"Reference","text":"component_count(mode::ExplorationMode, x) -> Int\n\nHow many parts x has, without materialising any of them.\n\nCalled on every node the explorer creates, so keep it O(1) — length, or a stored count. It decides two things: whether a row gets an expansion arrow at all, and where the … N more marker goes when a container is longer than the batch size. It must agree with what components actually yields.\n\nReturning 0 makes the value a leaf as far as the tree is concerned; see is_leaf for opting out of exploration entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.component_count"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.component_window-Tuple{ExplorationMode, Any, Int64, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"component_window(mode, x, start, limit) -> Vector{Component}\n\nThe components of x numbered start through start + limit - 1, in iteration order. Taking a window (rather than everything) is what keeps a million-element array browsable: the tree only ever holds one slice of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.component_window"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.components","page":"Reference","text":"components(mode::ExplorationMode, x) -> iterable of Component\n\nList the parts of x. This is the extension point: give your type a components(::Semantic, ::MyType) method to control how the explorer walks it.\n\nstruct RingBuffer\n    store::Vector{Float64}\n    len::Int\nend\n\nNarcissus.has_semantic_view(::RingBuffer) = true\nNarcissus.component_count(::Narcissus.Semantic, r::RingBuffer) = r.len\nNarcissus.components(::Narcissus.Semantic, r::RingBuffer) =\n    (Narcissus.Component(\"[$i]\", \"{}.store[$i]\", r.store[i]) for i in 1:r.len)\n\nThe contract\n\nIterable, not necessarily indexable. The explorer only ever takes a window out of the result — Iterators.drop then Iterators.take — so a lazy generator is the right shape and keeps a million-element container cheap. A Vector{Component} is fine for small values.\nStable order. Iteration order is display order, and it must not change between calls for an unchanged value: windowing, the … batch loader and \"re-read this node\" all assume that component n is the same component next time.\nAgrees with component_count. The count decides whether a row gets an expansion arrow and where the elision marker goes; a count that disagrees with the iterator produces a phantom … N more row.\nCheap to build. It is called every time a node is opened, and again for each further batch of a long container.\nDoes not throw. Wrap a failing lookup in AccessError so it becomes one visible row instead of an error. If the method itself throws, the explorer catches it and shows a single error row rather than dying, but you lose the other components.\n\nThe two modes\n\ncomponents(::Fields, x) is generic — it reads the struct fields Julia actually stores — and should not normally be specialised. components(::Semantic, x) falls back to it, so a plain struct looks the same in both modes and only types with something better to say need a method.\n\nOverload has_semantic_view to true alongside a Semantic method, or the m key will not offer the toggle.\n\nSee also component_count, is_leaf, Component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.components"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.copy_to_clipboard-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"copy_to_clipboard(text) -> Bool\n\nPut text on the system clipboard, returning whether anything actually took it. false means every available tool was missing or refused — the caller is expected to say so rather than claim success.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.copy_to_clipboard"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.current_node-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"The node under the cursor, or nothing when the tree is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.current_node"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.cycle_kinds!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"cycle_kinds!(tree) -> Symbol\n\nStep a module listing through all → functions → types → modules → values.\n\nA package's exported names are a jumble of four different things, and you are usually after one of them. Only rows directly under a module node are filtered — see flatten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.cycle_kinds!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.detail_spans-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"detail_spans(node, width) -> Vector{Span}\n\nThe right-hand pane: where the value lives, what it is, and how it prints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.detail_spans"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.diff_detail_spans-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64, Int64, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"diff_detail_spans(node, width, names) -> Vector{Span}\n\nThe right-hand pane for a comparison node: where each side lives, what each side is, and how each side prints — the left in one colour, the right in the other, matching the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.diff_detail_spans"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.diff_status","page":"Reference","text":"diff_status(d::Diff, mode=Semantic()) -> Symbol\n\nWhat happened between the two sides:\n\nStatus Meaning\n:same the two sides are the same, structurally\n:changed same shape, different contents\n:added present on the right only\n:removed present on the left only\n:type the two sides are values of unrelated types\n\nEquality is same_value, not plain isequal, so two deepcopy-equal mutable structs come back :same. It walks the value, so the explorer caches the answer per node rather than asking once per frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.diff_status"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.diff_tally-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"A one-line tally of what differs on screen, for the pane's title bar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.diff_tally"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.difference_counts-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"difference_counts(tree) -> NamedTuple\n\nHow many changed, added, removed and type-mismatched rows are on screen. A tally of what is visible, which grows as branches are opened — not a claim about the whole object, which would mean reading all of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.difference_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.dispatch_work!-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer}","page":"Reference","text":"dispatch_work!(m)\n\nHand whatever the last frame asked for to background tasks.\n\nScanning an array for NaNs or walking an object to size it takes time proportional to the data, and a frame must take time proportional to the screen. The renderer records what it wants (Narcissus.request!) and this spawns it, capped at MAX_IN_FLIGHT so that opening a wide row does not start a hundred walks at once. Results arrive as TaskEvents.\n\nThreads.@spawn does the work, so it genuinely overlaps only when Julia was started with more than one thread; on a single thread it still moves the cost out from between the layout and the paint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.dispatch_work!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.doc_binding-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"The Docs.Binding a value's documentation would be filed under.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.doc_binding"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.doc_object-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"doc_object(x, binding) -> the documentation to render\n\nEverything filed under binding, except where a narrower answer exists.\n\nA name collects docstrings from more than one thing. push! holds one for the function and one for each documented method; Widget holds one for the type and one for each documented constructor. Asking the docsystem for the binding asks for all of it, which answers \"what is a Widget?\" with the type's docstring followed by every constructor's — a page to scroll past to find the sentence you wanted.\n\nSo a Method takes the entry matching its own signature, and a Type takes the entry written with no signature at all, which is the one attached to the type. Each falls back to the whole binding when it has nothing of its own, because a constructor's docstring is a better answer than none; _docs! labels that case so the two are not mistaken for each other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.doc_object"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.doc_signature-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"doc_signature(x) -> Type\n\nThe argument-tuple type a documentation lookup should match, Union{} for anything but a Method — a bare binding wants every docstring filed under it.\n\nFor a method it is the method's own signature with the function's type dropped, which is the shape the docsystem keys signature-specific docstrings by. Handing that to Docs.doc is what makes ?push!(::Vector, ::Any) narrower than ?push!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.doc_signature"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.docstring-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"docstring(x; width=80, color=true) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nThe docstring attached to a function, type, module or method, rendered as Markdown, or nothing when there is none.\n\nReached through the binding rather than the value, which is how Julia stores docs: a generic function does not carry its own documentation, the name it is bound to does. Anonymous functions and closures have no such binding and come back nothing.\n\nA Method is looked up by signature and a Type by name alone, so each shows what was written about it rather than everything filed under the name — see doc_object.\n\nRendering goes through Julia's own Markdown writer at the given width, so headers, emphasis, lists and code blocks come out formatted rather than as raw **asterisks**. The formatting arrives as ANSI escapes, which Tachikoma.parse_ansi turns into styled spans — the detail pane shows what ?components shows in the REPL. Use strip_ansi for the bare text.\n\ncolor=false suppresses the Markdown writer's own colouring; syntax highlighting inside code blocks comes from Julia and stays either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.docstring"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.err_string-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"Render a thrown value — which need not be an Exception — as text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.err_string"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.expand_differences!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"expand_differences!(node, depth)\n\nOpen the branches that differ and leave the identical ones folded, down to depth extra levels. This is what a freshly opened narcissus(x, y) shows: the shape of the difference, with everything that matches out of the way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.expand_differences!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.expand_elided!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"expand_elided!(node) -> ObjNode\n\nReplace a … N more marker with the next window of its parent's children. Returns the parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.expand_elided!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.expand_recursive!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"expand_recursive!(node, depth)\n\nExpand node and everything beneath it down to depth extra levels. Elision markers are left alone — bulk-expanding is a convenience, not a way to accidentally materialise a million rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.expand_recursive!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.expandable-Tuple{ExplorationMode, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"Whether the tree should offer to expand x in mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.expandable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.exploration_mode-Tuple{ExplorationMode}","page":"Reference","text":"exploration_mode(x) -> ExplorationMode\n\nCoerce :semantic / :fields (or a mode instance) to an ExplorationMode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.exploration_mode"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.find_in_tail-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"find_in_tail(marker, predicate; budget, maxdepth, skip) -> Union{Nothing,ObjNode}\n\nSearch the components a … N more marker stands in for, and materialise only the branch that matches.\n\nCandidates are built detached from the tree and thrown away again, so a search that comes up empty leaves the container exactly as truncated as it found it — the alternative, expanding window after window as the walk goes, answers \"no match\" by having quietly grown a thousand rows onto the screen. A hit is then loaded for real, one window at a time up to the element that matched, and looked up again in the tree proper: the same walk, on nodes that are actually there for the cursor to land on.\n\nThe elements are read in limit-sized windows and every candidate costs a node of the shared budget, so a search over a million-element array stops where any other search stops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.find_in_tail"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.find_node-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"find_node(root, predicate; budget=SEARCH_BUDGET, maxdepth=32) -> Union{Nothing,ObjNode}\n\nWalk the tree in display order looking for a node predicate accepts, opening children as it goes.\n\nThis is the escalation behind / and a: the visible rows are searched first because that is instant, and only when nothing matches does the explorer go looking in the parts of the object it has not read yet. The walk is capped at budget nodes and maxdepth levels, because the whole point of the lazy tree is to never be obliged to read all of a large object.\n\nThe tail of a truncated container is searched as well — the elements behind a … N more marker are as much part of the object as the first hundred — but through find_in_tail, which does not pull them into the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.find_node"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.flatten-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"flatten(root; hide_same=false, kinds=:all) -> Vector{Row}\n\nThe currently visible rows, in display order.\n\nBoth filters drop rows, never components, so the elision arithmetic of a long container is untouched: a hidden subtree is simply never walked.\n\nhide_same drops the branches a comparison found identical.\nkinds keeps only one binding_kind beneath module nodes, which is how \"show me just the types this package defines\" is answered. It applies only under modules — elsewhere a listing of one kind is not a question anyone is asking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.flatten"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.forget_modules!-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"Drop the memoised module binding lists, so newly defined names show up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.forget_modules!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.has_method_doc-Tuple{Method}","page":"Reference","text":"has_method_doc(m::Method) -> Bool\n\nWhether a docstring is filed under this method's own signature, as opposed to one written for the function as a whole.\n\nThe distinction is worth drawing because docstring falls back to every docstring on the binding when a method has none of its own — useful, but not a description of this method, and the pane should say which it is showing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.has_method_doc"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.has_own_doc-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"has_own_doc(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether a docstring was written for x itself, as opposed to for its methods or its constructors. See doc_object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.has_own_doc"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.has_semantic_view","page":"Reference","text":"has_semantic_view(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x decomposes differently in the two ExplorationModes. true puts the m toggle on the row and makes the mode visible in the detail pane. Overload it to true alongside your components(::Semantic, …) method.\n\nThe default answers true for anything claimed by register_semantic!, so a trait-registered view needs no method here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.has_semantic_view"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.human_bytes-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Reference","text":"human_bytes(n) -> String\n\n1.4 MiB, 912 B. Fixed width enough to line up in a column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.human_bytes"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.hunt_anomaly!","page":"Reference","text":"hunt_anomaly!(tree, dir; from=tree.selected) -> Symbol\n\nThe anomaly counterpart of search!, with the same escalation: find the next NaN, Inf, missing, empty container, #undef or unreadable value, on screen if possible and in the unread object if not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.hunt_anomaly!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.identity_keys-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"identity_keys(x) -> Tuple\n\nThe object identities the explorer tracks for cycle detection. A plain value offers itself; a Diff offers both of its sides.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.identity_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.in_module-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"Whether the cursor is on, or inside, a module listing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.in_module"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.is_ancestor_value-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Narcissus.ObjNode}, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_ancestor_value(parent, v) -> Bool\n\ntrue when v is (identically) one of the values already on the path from the root. Self-referential structures are common enough — a node holding its parent, a graph, a closure over its own container — that walking into one blindly would hang the explorer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.is_ancestor_value"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.is_difference-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"Whether a status is worth stopping on when jumping between differences.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.is_difference"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.is_leaf-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_leaf(x) -> Bool\n\nValues the explorer refuses to descend into, in either mode, even though they have fields. Types and modules are the usual offenders: their internals are deep, cyclic, and almost never what you opened the explorer for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.is_leaf"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.jump!-Tuple{Any, Narcissus.ObjectTree, Int64, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"Move the cursor to the next visible row satisfying predicate, wrapping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.jump!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.kind_label-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"Plural label for a binding category, for the pane title.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.kind_label"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.kind_string-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"Human-readable description of what kind of thing a value is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.kind_string"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.load_children!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"load_children!(node) -> node\n\nMaterialise node's first window of children. Idempotent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.load_children!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.measure-Tuple{Symbol, Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"Compute one requested value. Runs off the main task, and touches no state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.measure"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.memory_spans","page":"Reference","text":"memory_spans(node) -> Vector{Tuple{String,Style}}\n\nThe memory column: retained size, a share bar, and the share as a percentage.\n\nThe share is what makes it readable — an absolute size tells you a node is large, but the proportion tells you whether it is the reason its parent is. Rendered as a right-aligned block of MEMORY_COLUMN_WIDTH cells so that sibling rows can be compared down the column rather than read one by one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.memory_spans"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.module_names-Tuple{Module}","page":"Reference","text":"module_names(m::Module; all=false) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe bindings of a module: its public API by default, everything it defines (internals, imports, generated names) when all.\n\nThe module's own name is dropped — every module exports itself, and a row that leads straight back to where you are is noise. Memoised; forget_modules! clears the memo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.module_names"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.multidocs-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"multidocs(binding) -> Vector\n\nThe docsystem's tables for a binding, one per module that wrote something under it — Base.push! collects an entry from every package that documents a method of it.\n\nReaching into Docs internals, so anything unexpected is answered with nothing found: the callers all have a sound fallback, and none of this is worth an error in a pane that is only trying to describe a value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.multidocs"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.n_components-Tuple{ExplorationMode, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"n_components(mode, x) -> Int\n\ncomponent_count, guarded: leaves and types with a throwing component_count report 0 rather than taking the app down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.n_components"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.narcissus-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"narcissus(x, y; names=(\"left\", \"right\"), expand=8, mode=:semantic, kwargs...)\n\nCompare two objects side by side and return the Diff under the cursor when you quit.\n\nThe tree opens with the differing branches expanded and the identical ones folded away, so the shape of the difference is the first thing you see. Each row is marked · identical, ~ changed, + added on the right, - removed, or ! for values of unrelated types; the left side of a changed value is printed in one colour and the right in the other. d and D jump between differences, and everything else — lazy expansion, m to switch between the semantic and field views, y to copy a path — works exactly as it does on a single object.\n\nnames labels the two sides and roots their path expressions, so pass the real variable names and the detail pane will show you old.config.lr against new.config.lr.\n\njulia> narcissus(before, after; names=(\"before\", \"after\"))\n\nA comparison bottoms out where the two sides stop lining up: values of differently named types are reported as a type change rather than zipped field by field. See Diff and diff_status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.narcissus"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.narcissus-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"narcissus(obj; name=\"obj\", limit=100, expand=1, mode=:semantic, kwargs...)\n\nOpen a terminal UI that walks obj field by field, and return the value sitting under the cursor when you quit.\n\nThe tree is built lazily: children are read only when a node is opened, cycles are detected and marked rather than followed, and containers are materialised limit elements at a time. That makes it safe to point at a recursive struct or a huge array.\n\nname — how the root is labelled, and the prefix of every path expression shown in the detail pane (so y yields something you can paste into the REPL).\nexpand — how many levels to open up front.\nlimit — container elements pulled in per batch.\nmode — :semantic (a Dict as its entries) or :fields (a Dict as the struct fields Julia stores). Any node can be flipped with m while you look. See components to teach Narcissus about your own types.\n\nExtra keyword arguments are forwarded to Tachikoma.app.\n\nPress ? inside the app for the full key list.\n\njulia> narcissus(rand(3, 3))\n\njulia> x = narcissus(model; name=\"model\")   # `x` is the row you left the cursor on\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.narcissus"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.next_difference!","page":"Reference","text":"next_difference!(tree, dir; from=tree.selected) -> Bool\n\nMove the cursor to the next visible row that is not identical on both sides. Unlike search! this does not escalate: the branches that differ were opened when the comparison did, and the ones still closed matched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.next_difference!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_anomaly-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"node_anomaly(node) -> Union{Nothing,Symbol}\n\nanomaly of a node's value, cached, computing it if it has not been computed yet. Arrays are scanned to answer this, which is why the answer is kept — and why the renderer asks anomaly_state instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_anomaly"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_bytes-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"node_bytes(node) -> Int\n\nbounded_size of a node's value, cached, computing it if needed. node._bytes_capped records whether the walk ran out of budget, in which case the number is a lower bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_measurand-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"What a node's size is a size of — the left side, for a comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_measurand"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_status-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"node_status(node) -> Symbol\n\ndiff_status of a comparison node, cached, or :none when the node holds an ordinary value. Cached because the status is asked for on every rendered frame but costs an isequal over the whole value to compute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_status"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_text-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"Flat text of a row, used for searching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_text"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.node_text_cheap-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"node_text_cheap(node) -> String\n\nWhat a row says, without paying for it.\n\nnode_text renders the value, which means a show call — fine for the rows on screen, ruinous for a deep walk that visits thousands of nodes it will throw away. This matches on the key and the type, plus the value only when some earlier render already cached it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.node_text_cheap"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.other_mode-Tuple{Semantic}","page":"Reference","text":"The other mode, for toggling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.other_mode"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.own_doc-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"own_doc(binding) -> Union{Nothing,Any}\n\nThe docstring written for a name itself — the one attached with no signature — or nothing when everything filed under that name belongs to a method or a constructor.\n\nThere is no public way to ask this. Docs.doc selects by signature and every signature is a supertype of Union{}, so asking it for a bare binding asks for all of them; the entry we want is the one the docsystem keys by Union{}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.own_doc"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.plain_show-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"plain_show(v; width, height) -> String\n\nThe show(io, MIME\"text/plain\"(), v) rendering used by the detail pane, under :limit at the given displaysize, so a million-element array prints the handful of rows that fit rather than a million.\n\nheight is what the caller can actually display — see refresh_detail!, which asks for the visible window plus a little, and asks again for more only when you scroll past it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.plain_show"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.preview-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"preview(node) -> String\n\nShort value rendering shown after the type on a tree row. Computed on first use and cached: only rows you actually look at pay for their show method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.preview"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.preview_spans","page":"Reference","text":"preview_spans(node, selected) -> Vector{Tuple{String,Style}}\n\nThe value column of a tree row, split into styled pieces. Ordinary values are one piece; a comparison is the left side, an arrow, and the right side, so the two are told apart by colour and not just by reading order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.preview_spans"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.print_diff-Tuple{IO, Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"print_diff([io], x, y; names=(\"left\", \"right\"), maxdepth=8, limit=20,\n           mode=:semantic, all=false)\n\nPrint what differs between two objects. The identical branches are left out — pass all=true to keep them — so the output is the difference and not the object.\n\nRows are marked · identical, ~ changed, + added on the right, - removed, ! unrelated types, in green when the two sides match and red when they do not.\n\njulia> print_diff(before, after; names=(\"before\", \"after\"))\nbefore ⇄ after::Run\n├─ ~ name::String \"exp-042\" → \"exp-043\"\n└─ ~ losses::Vector{Float64}\n   ├─ ~ [2]::Float64 1.87 → 1.8\n   └─ + [4]::Float64 1.02\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.print_diff"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.print_object-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"print_object([io], obj; name=\"obj\", maxdepth=3, limit=20, mode=:semantic)\n\nPrint the object tree instead of opening a terminal UI — for a log, a CI run, a notebook, or a quick look that does not deserve a full-screen app.\n\nmaxdepth is what keeps this honest: the tree loads children as it is walked, so an unbounded print would read all of a large object. Anything past the depth, or past limit elements of a container, is simply not visited.\n\njulia> print_object(model; maxdepth=2)\nmodel::Model\n└─ layers::Vector{Layer}\n   ├─ [1]::Layer = Layer([0.1 0.2; …], [0.0, 0.0])\n   └─ [2]::Layer = Layer([0.5 0.1], [0.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.print_object"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.realise_child!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Int64, Any, Int64, Union{Nothing, Narcissus.ObjNode}}","page":"Reference","text":"Load a truncated container up to index, and find the match inside it again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.realise_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.refresh_detail!-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer, Union{Nothing, Narcissus.ObjNode}, Int64, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"refresh_detail!(m, node, width, height)\n\nRebuild the detail pane, but only when it would say something different.\n\nRendering a value costs a show call, which for a large object is the most expensive thing in the frame — so the pane is rebuilt when the selection, the pane width or the object changes, and never merely because a frame was drawn. The show is also sized to the window you can see plus DETAIL_LOOKAHEAD lines; scrolling past that asks for a longer rendering, and only then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.refresh_detail!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.refresh_value!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"refresh_value!(node) -> Bool\n\nRe-read node's own value out of its parent, so a node that was created before the object mutated shows what is there now. Returns true if the value could be re-read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.refresh_value!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.register_semantic!-Tuple{Function, Function, Function}","page":"Reference","text":"register_semantic!(applies, count, parts)\n\nRegister a semantic view for values that dispatch cannot select on.\n\nMost types are served by adding a components method. Some ecosystems decide by trait instead of by type — Tables.istable(T) is true for a DataFrame without DataFrame sharing an abstract supertype you could dispatch on — and there is no method signature that captures them. Register the three functions instead:\n\napplies(x)::Bool — is this one of mine?\ncount(x)::Int — how many parts, cheaply (see component_count)\nparts(x) — the parts (see components)\n\nRegistrations are consulted in order, only for values no components method already claims, so they never shadow ordinary dispatch. Call this from an extension's __init__.\n\nNarcissus.register_semantic!(\n    x -> Tables.istable(typeof(x)),\n    x -> length(Tables.columnnames(Tables.columns(x))),\n    x -> (Component(String(n), \"Tables.getcolumn({}, :$n)\",\n                    Tables.getcolumn(Tables.columns(x), n))\n          for n in Tables.columnnames(Tables.columns(x))),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.register_semantic!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.reload!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"reload!(tree) -> Bool\n\nRe-read the node under the cursor: its cached preview, its child count and, if it was open, its children. Use it when the object is being mutated underneath the explorer — from a debugger prompt, or another task.\n\nThe subtree below the reloaded node collapses, since its children are new objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.reload!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.rename_root-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"Swap the root name of a path so it names the right-hand object instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.rename_root"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.render_detail!-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer, Tachikoma.Rect, Tachikoma.Buffer}","page":"Reference","text":"render_detail!(m, area, buffer)\n\nDraw the rows of the (already wrapped) detail pane that fit, and a scrollbar if there are more.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.render_detail!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.request!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree, Narcissus.ObjNode, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"request!(tree, node, kind)\n\nNote that node needs kind (:bytes or :anomaly) worked out, without working it out here.\n\nRendering a frame must stay proportional to the screen. Scanning an array for NaNs, or walking an object to size it, is proportional to the data, so the renderer records what it wants and the model computes it off the main task — see Narcissus.dispatch_work!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.request!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.reveal!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"reveal!(node)\n\nExpand every ancestor of node so that it becomes a visible row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.reveal!"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.root_node","page":"Reference","text":"Root node for value, displayed and path-rooted as name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.root_node"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.same_value-Tuple{ExplorationMode, Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"same_value(mode, a, b) -> Bool\n\nWhether a and b are the same as far as the explorer is concerned: isequal where that is meaningful (see trustworthy_equality), and a recursive walk over their components where it is not.\n\nThe walk is bounded by EQUALITY_BUDGET comparisons and EQUALITY_DEPTH levels. Exhausting either reports \"not the same\", which shows the branch as changed and leaves you to look — the honest answer when the question got too expensive to settle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.same_value"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.search!","page":"Reference","text":"search!(tree, query, dir; from=tree.selected, deep=true) -> Symbol\n\nFind query, returning :visible when a row already on screen matched, :revealed when the tree had to open the object up to find it, or :none.\n\nNames before values. A parent's preview contains the text of everything under it, so matching rendered values first would answer \"where is sigma?\" with the root row — technically a match, never the one you meant. Keys and types are tried first, on screen and then in the unread object; only when nothing is named that way does the value text of the visible rows count.\n\nThe deep walk also matches the values of leaf rows, which are cheap to render. It will not render a container's preview to search it — that is the expense the lazy tree exists to avoid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.search!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.select_node!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree, Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"Move the cursor to node if it is currently visible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.select_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.selected_value-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer}","page":"Reference","text":"The value currently under the cursor — what narcissus hands back on exit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.selected_value"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.semantic_trait-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"The registered view claiming x, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.semantic_trait"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.set_anomaly!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Union{Nothing, Symbol}}","page":"Reference","text":"Record an anomaly computed elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.set_anomaly!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.set_bytes!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, Tuple{Int64, Bool}}","page":"Reference","text":"Record a size computed elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.set_bytes!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.set_mode!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode, ExplorationMode}","page":"Reference","text":"set_mode!(node, mode) -> Bool\n\nShow node in a different ExplorationMode, discarding the children it had listed under the old one. Returns true if anything changed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.set_mode!"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.share_bar","page":"Reference","text":"A proportion as a short unicode bar, for eyeballing where the bytes went.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.share_bar"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.skip_for_size-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"skip_for_size(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is runtime machinery rather than the user's data, and so should be counted as nothing and never walked into.\n\nThis is not fussiness. Base.summarysize of a single Method is 111 MB: a method reaches its module, its specializations and from there most of the runtime. Measuring the twenty rows of a function's method list would walk that twenty times over, which is enough to hang or exhaust a session — and the answer was never a fact about anyone's data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.skip_for_size"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.squash-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"Collapse a multi-line rendering onto a single line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.squash"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.status_bar-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"status_bar(m, width) -> StatusBar\n\nThe key hints, trimmed from the right until they fit alongside whatever the right-hand side wants to say. A bar that overruns and collides with the path is worse than one that shows five hints instead of nine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.status_bar"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.status_marker-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"status_marker(status) -> (Char, Style)\n\nRow glyph and colour for a comparison status; :none draws nothing.\n\nGreen means the two sides match and red means they do not — one question, one colour, answered the same way everywhere the status appears. The glyph says how they fail to match, so + and - stay red rather than borrowing green for \"added\" and making green mean two different things.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.status_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.status_style-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"Green when the two sides match, red when they do not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.status_style"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.strip_ansi-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"Drop ANSI escapes, for testing what a rendering actually says.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.strip_ansi"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.switch_mode!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"switch_mode!(tree) -> Bool\n\nFlip the node under the cursor between its semantic and field views. A no-op on values with only one view — see has_semantic_view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.switch_mode!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.toggle!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"toggle!(node) -> Bool\n\nExpand or collapse node, loading its children on first expansion. Returns true if the tree shape changed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.toggle!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.toggle_memory!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjectTree}","page":"Reference","text":"toggle_memory!(tree)\n\nSwap the value column for what each row costs: its retained size, its share of its parent, and a bar to eyeball where the bytes went.\n\nSizes come from Base.summarysize, which walks the whole retained graph — so this is a key you press rather than a column that is always there, and the answer is cached per node once asked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.toggle_memory!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.toggle_mode!-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"toggle_mode!(node) -> Bool\n\nFlip node between its semantic and field views. Only values that answer has_semantic_view with true have two views to flip between.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.toggle_mode!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.truncate_text-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"Truncate s to n display columns, marking the cut with an ellipsis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.truncate_text"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.trustworthy_equality-Tuple{Type}","page":"Reference","text":"trustworthy_equality(T) -> Bool\n\nWhether isequal on T answers the question a diff is actually asking.\n\nIt does not for a mutable struct, where == falls back to === and two field-by-field identical objects compare unequal — nor for a container of them, since == on a Vector defers to its elements. For those, Narcissus compares component by component instead, which is both the slower path and the one that tells you something. Types that define their own == (numbers, strings, arrays of bits types) are taken at their word.\n\nMemoised per type: the answer needs which, a runtime method lookup costing microseconds, and it is asked once per value comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.trustworthy_equality"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.type_string-Tuple{Narcissus.ObjNode}","page":"Reference","text":"Full type name of a node's value, or a marker for the synthetic node kinds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.type_string"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.warm!-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer}","page":"Reference","text":"warm!(model) -> model\n\nRender one frame off-screen, before the terminal is handed over.\n\nEverything expensive about the first frame happens here rather than behind the alternate screen: the show method of a value nobody has printed before, the preview of every row that will be visible, the detail pane. Both are cached on the nodes, so the app does not pay for it twice.\n\nThe point is not the speed, it is the way out. Inside the app loop the terminal is in raw mode, which makes ^C a byte waiting to be read rather than a signal — and a value whose show takes a minute is then a minute you cannot interrupt, in front of a screen that has not drawn yet. Out here it is an ordinary computation and ^C is an ordinary interrupt.\n\nAnything else that goes wrong is left for the app, which draws each row inside its own error handling and will report it in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.warm!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.wrap_spans-Tuple{Vector{Tachikoma.Span}, Int64}","page":"Reference","text":"wrap_spans(spans, width) -> Vector{Vector{Tuple{String,Style}}}\n\nLay styled text out into lines of at most width columns, breaking at spaces where possible and mid-word only when a word cannot fit on a line of its own. Explicit newlines in the span text are honoured.\n\nThe point of doing this ourselves is that the result can be cached. A paragraph widget re-wraps its whole content on every frame; the detail pane's content changes only when the selection or the pane width does, so wrapping once and drawing the visible slice is the difference between a per-frame cost proportional to the rendered value and one proportional to the screen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.wrap_spans"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Tachikoma.update!-Tuple{Narcissus.Explorer, Tachikoma.TaskEvent}","page":"Reference","text":"update!(m::Explorer, e::TaskEvent)\n\nTake delivery of a background measurement. Writing the result here, on the main task, is what keeps it out of a data race with the walk that produced it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Tachikoma.update!"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#Narcissus.@narcissus-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"@narcissus expr\n@narcissus expr₁ expr₂\n@narcissus expr key=value...\n\nExplore (or compare) the value of an expression, naming the root after the expression itself.\n\nThe point is the paths. narcissus(model.layers[2]) roots every path at a generic obj, so y hands you obj.weights — true, but not something you can paste anywhere. The macro captures the source text instead:\n\njulia> @narcissus model.layers[2]      # `y` now yields model.layers[2].weights\njulia> @narcissus before after         # rooted at `before` and `after`\njulia> @narcissus model mode=:fields   # keyword arguments pass straight through\n\nAn explicit name or names wins over the captured text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Narcissus.@narcissus"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#interface","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Narcissus walks an object by asking it, repeatedly, \"what are you made of?\". Four functions answer that question, and all four are yours to overload:\n\nFunction Answers\ncomponents what are the parts?\ncomponent_count how many parts, without building any?\nhas_semantic_view do you decompose differently in the two modes?\nis_leaf should the explorer refuse to go inside you?\n\nYou need a method for a type only when the default is wrong. The default reads struct fields, which is right for most things.","title":"The decomposition interface"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#The-two-modes","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Every node carries an ExplorationMode, and m flips it:\n\nSemantic — what the value means. A Dict is its entries, a Vector is its elements, a Set is its members.\nFields — what the value is. A Dict is slots, keys, vals, ndel, count, age, idxfloor, maxprobe; a Vector is ref and size.\n\n▾ d::Dict{Symbol, Int64} = Dict(:a=>1, :b=>2)      ▾ d::Dict{Symbol, Int64} ·fields = …\n├─ :a::Int64 = 1                                   ├─▸slots::Memory{UInt8} = UInt8[0xb5, …\n└─ :b::Int64 = 2                                   ├─▸keys::Memory{Symbol} = [:a, :b, #un…\n                                                   ├─▸vals::Memory{Int64} = [1, 2, 140644…\n       semantic                                    ├─ ndel::Int64 = 0\n                                                   ├─ count::Int64 = 2\n                                                   └─ …          fields\n\ncomponents(::Fields, x) is generic and reads the fields Julia stores, so you almost never write one. components(::Semantic, x) falls back to the field view, which is why a plain struct looks identical in both modes — only types with something better to say need a method.\n\nThe mode propagates: children opened under a field-mode node inherit it, so dropping into fields means you meant to spelunk the storage below a value and not merely at it.","title":"The two modes"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Types-and-modules","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Two built-in views exist because the same question — \"what are you made of?\" — has a good answer for things that are not values:\n\nA type decomposes into its fields' types, which turns narcissus(SomeType) into a readable map of an unfamiliar package:\n\nRun::type = Run\n├─ name::type = String\n├─ cfg::type = Cfg\n│  ├─ lr::type = Float64\n│  └─ tags::type = Vector{Symbol}\n└─ losses::type = Vector{Float64}\n\nAnything without a definite layout — an abstract type, a UnionAll, a Union — has nothing to show and is a leaf. Paths are fieldtype(fieldtype(T, :cfg), :lr), which evaluates.\n\nA function decomposes into its methods, keyed by their argument signatures:\n\n▾ components::function = components (generic function with 13 methods)\n├─ (::Semantic, ::Module)::Method = components(::Semantic, m::Module) @ Narcissus …\n├─ (::Semantic, ::Diff)::Method   = components(::Semantic, d::Diff) @ Narcissus …\n└─ (::Semantic, ::Any)::Method    = components(::Semantic, x) @ Narcissus …\n\nIts field view is what the closure captured — usually nothing, and interesting exactly when it is not. The detail pane shows the function's docstring, reached through its binding rather than its value, because that is where Julia files documentation. Types and modules get theirs the same way.\n\nA module is the one place the two modes map onto something people already have names for:\n\nMode A module is…\nSemantic what it offers — names(m), the public API\nFields everything it defines — internals, imports, the lot\n\nSo narcissus(SomePackage) is a browsable API listing, and m drops you into the internals. Because a package's names are a jumble of four different kinds of thing, f narrows the listing to one at a time — functions, then types, then modules, then plain values — using binding_kind. The module's own name is filtered out (every module exports itself, and a row leading back to where you already are is noise), and the binding lists are memoised — press r on a module node after defining something new to re-read it.","title":"Types and modules"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#A-worked-example","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"A ring buffer stores more than it holds. Its fields are honest but useless: you want to see the three live elements, not a five-element backing array and an integer.\n\nusing Narcissus\n\nstruct RingBuffer\n    store::Vector{Float64}\n    len::Int\nend\n\nNarcissus.has_semantic_view(::RingBuffer) = true\n\nNarcissus.component_count(::Narcissus.Semantic, r::RingBuffer) = r.len\n\nNarcissus.components(::Narcissus.Semantic, r::RingBuffer) =\n    (Narcissus.Component(\"[$i]\", \"{}.store[$i]\", r.store[i]) for i in 1:r.len)\n\n▾ rb::RingBuffer = RingBuffer([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 0.0, 0.0], 3)\n├─ [1]::Float64 = 1.0          @ rb.store[1]\n├─ [2]::Float64 = 2.0          @ rb.store[2]\n└─ [3]::Float64 = 3.0          @ rb.store[3]\n\nThree methods, and the type now behaves like a first-class container: it elides when long, compares against another ring buffer, and flips back to store/len with m.","title":"A worked example"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#What-goes-in-a-Component","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"A row is built out of exactly four things.\n\n├─▸ weights::Matrix{Float64} = [0.1 0.2; 0.3 0.4]\n    └ key    └ from `value`    └ from `value`","title":"What goes in a Component"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#key-—-the-label-in-the-left-column","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Short, and specific enough to tell siblings apart. Following the built-in conventions keeps a custom type from looking foreign next to a Vector:\n\nContainer key\nstruct field the bare name, \"weights\"\nsequence \"[3]\", or \"[2, 5]\" for a multidimensional index\ndictionary the key as Julia code — repr(k), so \":name\"\n\nThe key shares a row with the type and the value, so keep it to a few characters where you can. It is also what / searches, along with the type and the rendered value.","title":"key — the label in the left column"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#template-—-the-path-expression","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"The Julia expression that reaches this component from its parent, with {} standing in for the parent's own path:\n\nTemplate Path it produces\n\"{}.weights\" model.layers[2].weights\n\"{}[3]\" v[3]\n\"{}[:name]\" d[:name]\n\"collect({})[2]\" collect(s)[2]\n\"{}\" the parent's own path\n\nPaths compose as the tree descends, and the result is what y copies to the clipboard — so make it something that can be pasted into the REPL and evaluated. When no expression reaches the component, use \"{}\": naming the parent is honest, inventing an accessor that does not work is not.","title":"template — the path expression"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#value-—-the-thing-itself","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Whatever the component holds. Two stand-ins exist for the awkward cases, and reaching for them is much better than throwing:\n\nUndef — a field that is declared but never assigned.\nAccessError — a value that could not be retrieved.","title":"value — the thing itself"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#kind-—-how-the-key-is-coloured","page":"The decomposition interface","text":":field, :index or :key. Purely cosmetic: it tells the reader at a glance whether they are looking at a struct, a sequence or a mapping.","title":"kind — how the key is coloured"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#The-contract","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"components must be:\n\nIterable, not necessarily indexable. The explorer only ever takes a window out of the result — Iterators.drop then Iterators.take — so a lazy generator is the right shape and keeps a million-element container cheap.\nStably ordered. Iteration order is display order, and it must not change between calls for an unchanged value. Windowing, the … batch loader and \"re-read this node\" all assume component n is the same component next time.\nIn agreement with component_count. The count decides whether a row gets an expansion arrow and where the elision marker goes. A count that disagrees with the iterator produces a phantom … N more row.\nCheap to build. It is called every time a node is opened, and again for each further batch of a long container.\nNon-throwing. Wrap a failing lookup in AccessError so it becomes one visible row. If the method itself throws, the explorer catches it and shows a single error row rather than dying — but you lose the other components.","title":"The contract"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Opting-out-entirely","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"is_leaf stops the explorer at a value in both modes:\n\nNarcissus.is_leaf(::MyOpaqueHandle) = true\n\nType, Symbol, AbstractString and AbstractChar are leaves out of the box.","title":"Opting out entirely"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Trait-based-views","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Some ecosystems decide what a value is by trait rather than by type, and there is no method signature that captures them — Tables.istable(typeof(x)) is true for a DataFrame without DataFrame sharing any abstract supertype you could dispatch on.\n\nregister_semantic! takes the three functions instead:\n\nfunction __init__()\n    Narcissus.register_semantic!(\n        x -> Tables.istable(typeof(x)),                       # applies\n        x -> length(Tables.columnnames(Tables.columns(x))),   # count\n        x -> (Component(String(n), \"Tables.getcolumn({}, :\\$n)\",\n                        Tables.getcolumn(Tables.columns(x), n))\n              for n in Tables.columnnames(Tables.columns(x))),\n    )\nend\n\nRegistrations are consulted only for values that no components method already claims, so they never shadow ordinary dispatch — and a registered view answers has_semantic_view true without needing a method of its own.","title":"Trait-based views"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Comparison-comes-along-for-free","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"narcissus(x, y) wraps the pair in a Diff, which is itself a value that implements this interface — it decomposes by zipping the components of both sides together. A type that defines components(::Semantic, …) therefore compares element by element with no further work:\n\n~ rb ⇄ rb2::RingBuffer = RingBuffer([1.0, 2.0, …  →  RingBuffer([1.0, 9.0, …\n├─ · [1]::Float64 = 1.0\n├─ ~ [2]::Float64 = 2.0 → 9.0\n└─ + [3]::Float64 = 3.0\n\nPositional pairing is the default. Dictionaries and sets pair by key and by membership instead, so a reordered Dict does not read as entirely changed.","title":"Comparison comes along for free"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Structural-equality","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Deciding whether a row is :same cannot just call isequal: for a mutable struct, == falls back to ===, and two field-by-field identical objects compare unequal. same_value uses isequal where it is meaningful and walks components where it is not — see trustworthy_equality for how that call is made, and EQUALITY_BUDGET for the bound that keeps a pathological object from hanging the explorer.","title":"Structural equality"},{"category":"section","location":"20-interface/#Reference","page":"The decomposition interface","text":"Full docstrings for all of the above live on the Reference page: components, component_count, has_semantic_view, is_leaf, Component, ExplorationMode, Undef, AccessError, register_semantic!, module_names, binding_kind, docstring.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Narcissus","page":"Narcissus","text":"A terminal UI for staring at your Julia objects. Point it at a value and walk its fields, elements and entries one level at a time — or point it at two and see what changed.\n\nBuilt on Tachikoma.jl, with the detail pane owing a debt to About.jl and the comparison mode to ObjectDiff.jl.\n\nThis project was vibe coded in its entirety — every line written by an LLM from conversational prompts.\n\nusing Narcissus\n\nnarcissus(model)                                     # explore one object\nnarcissus(before, after; names=(\"before\", \"after\"))  # compare two\n@narcissus model.layers[2]                           # …and keep the real path\nprint_object(model)                                  # or just print it\n\n╭─  object  ───────────────────────  13 rows  ─╮╭─  detail  ───────────────────  [1]  ─╮\n│  ▾run::Run = Run(\"exp-042\", Config(0.003, 50…││path     run.losses[1]                │\n│  ├─ name::String = \"exp-042\"                 ││type     Float64                      │\n│  ├─▾config::Config = Config(0.003, 50, [:bas…││kind     primitive                    │\n│  │ ├─ lr::Float64 = 0.003                    ││parts    0                            │\n│  │ ├─ epochs::Int64 = 50                     ││sizeof   8 bytes                      │\n│  │ └─▸tags::Vector{Symbol} = [:baseline, :ad…││super    AbstractFloat                │\n│  ├─▾losses::Vector{Float64} = [2.41, 1.87, 1…││                                      │\n│▌ │ ├─ [1]::Float64 = 2.41                    ││───────────────────────────────────── │\n│  │ ├─ [2]::Float64 = 1.87                    ││2.41                                  │\n│  │ └─ [3]::Float64 = 1.55                    ││                                      │\n│  └─▾notes::Dict{Symbol, An… = Dict{Symbol, A…││                                      │\n│    ├─ :host::String = \"gpu-03\"               ││                                      │\n│    └─ :seed::Int64 = 1234                    ││                                      │\n│                                              ││                                      │\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────╯╰──────────────────────────────────────╯\n ↑↓ move ←→ fold ⏎ toggle / search a anomaly M memory m view y path       run.losses[1]","title":"Narcissus"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-a-value-back-out","page":"Narcissus","text":"narcissus returns whatever the cursor is on when you quit, so the explorer doubles as a picker:\n\njulia> layer = narcissus(model; name=\"model\")   # navigate, then press q\njulia> layer.weights\n\nThe name keyword is both the label of the root row and the prefix of every path expression in the detail pane — pass the variable's real name and y gives you something you can paste straight back into the REPL. Better, let @narcissus capture it:\n\njulia> @narcissus model.layers[2]     # `y` now yields model.layers[2].weights","title":"Getting a value back out"},{"category":"section","location":"#Finding-things","page":"Narcissus","text":"/ looks at the rows already on screen first, and when nothing matches it opens the object up and keeps looking — so a field buried six levels down is one keystroke away:\n\n/ sigma_noise ⏎     →  found in o.a.inner.sigma_noise\n\nNames and types are tried before rendered values, because a parent's preview contains the text of everything beneath it and would otherwise answer every query with the root row. The walk is bounded by SEARCH_BUDGET nodes: never being obliged to read all of a large object is the whole point of the lazy tree.\n\na hunts anomaly rows the same way — the next NaN, Inf, missing, empty container, #undef or unreadable value, wherever it is.","title":"Finding things"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-the-memory-went","page":"Narcissus","text":"M swaps the value column for what each row costs: its retained size (Base.summarysize, not the shallow sizeof), its share of its parent, and a bar.\n\n▌ ▾m::M = 391.0 KiB ▕████████▏ 100%\n  ├─▸small::Vector{Int64} =      64 B ▕········▏   0%\n  ├─▸big::Vector{Float64} = 391.0 KiB ▕████████▏ 100%\n  └─ tag::String =      10 B ▕········▏   0%\n\nAn absolute size tells you a node is large; the share tells you whether it is the reason its parent is.\n\nSizes appear as … and fill in a moment later. Measuring walks the object, so it happens on a background task rather than in the frame — see bounded_size, which also refuses to follow modules, types and methods (Base.summarysize of a single Method is 111 MB) and stops after a fixed number of objects, reporting >1.2 MiB when the answer is a lower bound. The NaN and Inf flags are computed the same way, for the same reason.","title":"Where the memory went"},{"category":"section","location":"#Types-and-modules","page":"Narcissus","text":"narcissus(SomeType) shows the fields and their types, recursively — a readable map of an unfamiliar package. narcissus(SomePackage) shows a module's public API, m drops into everything it defines, and f narrows the listing to functions, types, submodules or values in turn.\n\nA function decomposes into its methods, and its docstring goes in the detail pane — as do a type's and a module's. See the interface page for how all of this is just components methods.","title":"Types and modules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Printing-without-a-terminal","page":"Narcissus","text":"print_object and print_diff render the same trees to any IO — for a log, a CI run, or a notebook. Both take a maxdepth, which is what keeps them from reading all of a large object.\n\nprint_diff and Diff are not exported — tree and diff packages commonly export names of their own that would collide — so reach for them as Narcissus.print_diff and Narcissus.Diff.\n\njulia> Narcissus.print_diff(before, after; names=(\"before\", \"after\"))","title":"Printing without a terminal"},{"category":"section","location":"#Comparing-two-objects","page":"Narcissus","text":"narcissus(x, y) opens with the differing branches expanded and the identical ones folded away, so the shape of the difference is the first thing you see.\n\n╭─  comparison  ──────────────────  ~8 +3 -1  ─╮╭─  detail  ──────────────────  name  ─╮\n│  ▾~ before ⇄ after::Run = Run(\"exp-042\", Co…█││status   changed                      │\n│▌ ├─ ~ name::String = \"exp-042\" → \"exp-043\"  █││before   before.name                  │\n│  ├─▾~ config::Config = Config(0.003, 50, [:…█││after    after.name                   │\n│  │ ├─ ~ lr::Float64 = 0.003 → 0.001         █││parts    0                            │\n│  │ ├─ · epochs::Int64 = 50                  █││                                      │\n│  │ └─▾~ tags::Vector{Symbol} = [:baseline, …█││─── before ────────────────────────── │\n│  │   ├─ · [1]::Symbol = :baseline           █││type     String                       │\n│  │   ├─ · [2]::Symbol = :adamw              █││\"exp-042\"                             │\n│  │   └─ + [3]::Symbol = :warmup             █││                                      │\n│  ├─▾~ losses::Vector{Float6… = [2.41, 1.87,…█││─── after ─────────────────────────── │\n│  │ ├─ · [1]::Float64 = 2.41                 █││type     String                       │\n│  │ ├─ ~ [2]::Float64 = 1.87 → 1.8           █││\"exp-043\"                             │\n│  │ ├─ · [3]::Float64 = 1.55                 █││                                      │\n│  │ └─ + [4]::Float64 = 1.02                 █││                                      │\n│  └─▾~ notes::Dict{Symbol, A… = Dict{Symbol,…█││                                      │\n│    ├─ - :host::String = \"gpu-03\"            █││                                      │\n│    ├─ · :seed::Int64 = 1234                 │││                                      │\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────╯╰──────────────────────────────────────╯\n ↑↓ move d next diff f fold same e expand / search m view y path ? help     before.name\n\nMarker Colour Meaning\n· green the two sides are identical\n~ red changed\n+ red present on the right only\n- red present on the left only\n! red values of unrelated types\n\nGreen means the two sides match and red means they do not; within a changed row the left side is printed in one colour and the right in the other. d and D jump between differences.\n\nEquality is same_value, not isequal: for a mutable struct == falls back to ===, so two field-by-field identical objects would otherwise read as completely changed. Dictionaries and sets pair by key and by membership, everything else positionally.\n\nA comparison is not a special mode bolted on — it is a Diff value that implements the same decomposition interface everything else does, which is why laziness, elision, cycle detection and the m toggle all work on it unchanged.","title":"Comparing two objects"},{"category":"section","location":"#Two-views-of-a-value","page":"Narcissus","text":"m flips the row under the cursor between what a value means (a Dict is its entries) and what it is (a Dict is slots, keys, vals, ndel, count, age, idxfloor, maxprobe). Rows showing storage are tagged ·fields, and the mode propagates to whatever you open beneath them.\n\nStart in either with narcissus(x; mode=:fields). See The decomposition interface for teaching your own types how to decompose.","title":"Two views of a value"},{"category":"section","location":"#Keys","page":"Narcissus","text":"Key Action\n↑ k / ↓ j Move up / down\n→ l Expand, or step into the first child\n← h Collapse, or jump to the parent\nEnter / Space Toggle the row under the cursor\ne / c Expand (differences, or two levels) / collapse\nPgUp / PgDn Jump a page\ng / G First / last row\n/ Search — on screen, then into the unread object\nn / N Next / previous match\na / A Next / previous anomaly\nM Show retained size instead of values\nd / D Next / previous difference (when comparing)\nf Narrow the view — hide matched branches, or filter a module\nm Switch between the semantic and field views\nTab Move focus between the tree and the detail pane\ny / Y Copy the path expression / the printed value\nr Re-read the selected node from the live object\n? Key help\nq / Ctrl+C Quit, returning the selected value\n\nThe mouse works too: click to select, click again to fold, wheel to scroll, and drag the border between the panes to resize them — right-click it to restore the default. Ctrl+T opens Tachikoma's theme picker.","title":"Keys"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-the-traversal-behaves","page":"Narcissus","text":"An object explorer meets a lot of values that do not want to be explored, so the traversal is deliberately defensive:\n\nLazy. Children are read only when a node is opened, so pointing Narcissus at a large object graph costs nothing until you walk into it.\nCycles are detected by identity against the values on the current path and marked ↺ already shown above instead of being followed.\nLong containers arrive limit elements at a time, with a … row at the end that pulls in the next batch when you open it.\nUndefined fields show as #undef, and a getindex, getfield or show method that throws becomes a visible marker rather than a stack trace.\nExpensive questions are bounded. Structural equality gives up after a set number of comparisons; a deep search gives up after SEARCH_BUDGET nodes. Neither hangs on a pathological object.","title":"How the traversal behaves"},{"category":"section","location":"#Path-expressions","page":"Narcissus","text":"Every row knows how to name itself as Julia code:\n\nContainer Path\nstruct field run.config\nodd field name getfield(x, Symbol(\"odd name\"))\nvector v[3]\nmatrix m[2, 3]\ndictionary d[:key]\nset collect(s)[3]\nstruct storage d.slots, v.ref\n\ny copies the one under the cursor to the clipboard.","title":"Path expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-next","page":"Narcissus","text":"The decomposition interface — teaching Narcissus your own types, and the two modes in detail.\nReference — the full API.","title":"Where to next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Narcissus","text":"Tachikoma.jl — the terminal UI framework everything here is built on.\nAbout.jl — about(x) prints a rich description of a value: what it is, what it costs, how it is laid out. Narcissus' detail pane is that idea made navigable, one row at a time.\nObjectDiff.jl — the recursive comparison that narcissus(x, y) grew out of, including its insight that == is the wrong question to ask about two mutable structs.","title":"Acknowledgements"}]
}
